{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · one plane","loop":56,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.570569891,-0.11784261,-0.313066464,0.1396608]},{"t":0.16,"box":[-0.570569891,-0.11784261,-0.313066464,0.1396608]},{"t":0.19,"box":[-0.270654545,-0.134290909,-0.0402545455,0.0961090909]},{"t":0.67,"box":[-0.270654545,-0.134290909,-0.0402545455,0.0961090909]},{"t":0.7,"box":[-0.278181818,-0.141818182,0.196363636,0.109090909]},{"t":0.84,"box":[-0.278181818,-0.141818182,0.196363636,0.109090909]},{"t":0.87,"box":[0.1439559,-0.112407736,0.573942736,0.134225918]}],"stops":[0.16,0.33,0.5,0.67,0.84],"beats":[{"title":"Two fingers","text":"Spread two fingers on a [map](#lit:0) and it grows; twist them and it [turns](#lit:1). The phone reads one number off the [fingers](#lit:2,3): the [line between them](#lit:4) after, divided by the same line before, k·e^(iα).\n\nIts logarithm, ln k + iα, puts the pinch on one axis and the twist on the other. Adding is the same in either order, so neither gesture gets in the other's way."},{"title":"One plane","text":"Divide every point by the thumb and take the logarithm: ln(z/H) = ln(d/H) + iθ, the direction up and the distance across. A [writer's grid](#lit:6,7) bends onto it.\n\n[Circles about the eye](#lit:8) become upright lines, one in two, [one thumb](#lit:9), two and four thumbs, evenly spaced: nothing nests. The [ray at 45°](#lit:10) becomes a level line."},{"title":"Reading up, writing across","text":"Turn the world and every point slides up; make it larger, or hold up a smaller thumb, and every point slides across, nothing else moving. [Turn then scale](#lit:11,12) ends where [scale then turn](#lit:13,14) does.\n\nUp and down is what the reader holds. Across is the writer's: an amount in thumbs, whose zero, the size of anything, no reading fixes."},{"title":"Two corners, two mirrors","text":"Swap a reading for its reciprocal, v/h for h/v, and a [point](#lit:15) reflects in the [level line at 45°](#lit:10) to [here](#lit:17,16). Swap a distance for its reciprocal about the thumb and it reflects in the [upright line at one thumb](#lit:9) to [here](#lit:19,18).\n\nThe two mirrors move on different axes, the look and the thumb, and cross at [one point](#lit:20): on the look, one thumb out, where the heat is hottest."},{"title":"Distance as a turn","text":"Euler's plane runs without end both ways across. The thumb's own lay of distance is bounded: the writer writes it (2/π)·arctan(d/H), which against the plane's coordinate is [½ + gd(x)/π](#lit:23), Mercator's function.\n\nLaid by it, the same [grid](#lit:6,7,8) presses the far field against the right edge, every direction's horizon at once: [one, two, four and eight thumbs](#lit:24,25,26,27) at 0.5, 0.70, 0.84 and 0.92."},{"title":"Where it stops","text":"In space the directions are a sphere, and no flat chart keeps both jobs. On [Lambert's map](#lit:35,36,39) every cell keeps its area as the look turns, so the heat stays flat, but the cells bend: the [old look's place](#lit:41,42) is stretched 1/cos(φ/2). On the [stereographic map](#lit:37,38,40) every cell keeps its angles, but the cells grow, and the heat at its rim is [a quarter of the middle's](#lit:30,31,32,33,34).\n\nWriting still slides across in space; turning no longer slides. The plane does both only because a line times a circle is flat. [The essay, §9](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/18-read-and-write-on-one-plane.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.490909091,0.490909091,-0.490909091,-0.490909091],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":4},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph"